{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner of the Muslim ruler Nur ad-Din. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply knowledgeable about Islamic culture and politics. He eventually gained freedom through a ransom payment and returned to lead his county with a pragmatic approach that often favored diplomacy over immediate military conflict.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed power while still young yet his reign began under difficult circumstances because his father died in battle. The young count faced immediate threats from neighboring Muslim forces and internal instability within the crusader states. His capture occurred when he ventured out against superior numbers and this event removed him from political life for a decade. During this time he learned Arabic and studied Muslim customs which later influenced his diplomatic strategies upon release.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nThe count became regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. Raymond managed state affairs with great care and he sought to maintain peace with Saladin to protect the fragile kingdom. His leadership style clashed with more aggressive factions like the Knights Templar or the court party led by Guy of Lusignan. These groups preferred direct confrontation while Raymond understood the military weakness of the crusader states at that specific historical moment. He acted as a stabilizing force during a period filled with succession crises and external threats.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nHis later years were marked by increasing tension between peace advocates and war hawks within the kingdom. The disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 validated his warnings about engaging Saladin in open field combat without adequate preparation. Raymond survived the battle but the defeat led to the loss of Jerusalem and most crusader territories. He died shortly after these events and his death ended a significant era of diplomatic engagement in the Latin East. Historians view him as one of the most capable leaders of his time due to his foresight and understanding of regional dynamics. His efforts to preserve the kingdom through negotiation rather than reckless warfare remain a notable aspect of his historical record.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.8253e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.004318668}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.004705755}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.009585518}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.059947e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.861027e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0014601873}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.04653452}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.004490429}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.11163949}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.047560062}, {"token": " of", "